# Homepage service layout — auto-generated by the docker_services post-deploy hook.
# Vault-escape note: vault values rendered here pass through unchanged (this is NOT a
# `docker compose` interpolated file), so no `| replace('$','$$')` is needed.
# Source of truth: the group_vars/vps.yml docker_services list + this template.
# See docs/interfaces.md for the interface model.
# See docs/interfaces.md for the 6-tier interface model.
#
# Family dashboard split on kogler.si / home.kogler.si:
#   - Family groups (Photos/Media/Files/Docs/Chat/Git/AI/Tools/Accounts) tile the LIVE,
#     publicly-routed apps. Health dots = Homepage `siteMonitor` probes of each app's public
#     URL — probe-based, deliberately NO docker socket (on the VPS it sees only VPS containers).
#     A tile's endpoint comes from a group_var (homepage_*_url), so the dashboard follows the
#     edge route tables instead of duplicating them.
#   - Domen · technical: tailnet-only admin dashboards (stats/logs/csui/traefik/auto/dsh/pi-dev
#     + headscale control plane), hidden from the family group by `homepage_show_technical`
#     (default true). Grafana (stats) is the technical dashboard — never a family tile.
#   - Soon: apps with NO backend at all render as description-only tiles (href '#') so the
#     family sees the roadmap without dead links; gated by `homepage_show_soon` (default true).
#     Anything that IS deployed belongs in its family group with a real endpoint — the roadmap
#     group is never a place a live service can land.
#
# Probe policy: siteMonitor is used ONLY for FQDNs the homepage container can actually
# resolve+reach (the public CNAMEs). Tailnet-only names (stats/logs/csui/sec/traefik/auto/
# dsh/pi-dev/ai) are NOT probed — the container is not a tailnet node, so it would render
# false-red dots. Their health plane is Grafana itself.

# Manual HA failover buttons (docs/smart-home-failover.md): href cards call the
# ha-failover-api endpoint on oldsrv (?token= GET; POST with X-Failover-Token also works).
# The trigger is IP-only: it moves the HA VIP and starts/stops the standby container — nothing
# physical moves, Homematic rides the cloud HAP. `failover_api_url` is the oldsrv HOME-VLAN
# address, so the card works from a device on the home network and reaches nothing from away.
# Rendered only when the group_var homepage_failover_button is true, so a button is never dead.
# ⚠ STATE, measured 2026-10-19 (docs/smart-home-failover.md §Intent vs reality): the standby is
# UNDEPLOYED — oldsrv runs no `home-assistant-standby`/`keepalived` container, and the tag the compose
# pins is not on that host. So "standby start" below was the lie this line now corrects: a forward click
# runs `compose up -d`, which CREATES both containers under time pressure and stalls on a registry pull if
# the image is missing. The descriptions say what the click does TODAY. The cold create
# (`--tags ha_failover` on oldsrv, owner-window) is what would make them read like a standby start again.
{% if homepage_failover_button %}
- Smart Home Failover:
    - HA Forward Takeover:
        icon: sh-home-assistant
        href: "{{ failover_api_url }}/failover?token={{ lookup('community.general.onepassword', 'ha-failover_api', field='credential', vault=op_vault) }}"
        description: Pi → oldsrv — VIP move, then create+start the standby (none exists yet: this click creates it)
    - HA Reverse Failback:
        icon: sh-home-assistant
        href: "{{ failover_api_url }}/failback?token={{ lookup('community.general.onepassword', 'ha-failover_api', field='credential', vault=op_vault) }}"
        description: oldsrv → Pi — standby down, then VIP back (no takeover has ever been drilled)
{% endif %}

{% set d = domain_local | default('kogler.si') %}

